From 32206064f69da1a8f1c96052150d78dcd71da18e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 19:40:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban overlap: the key-family exemption covers a
 creator's own cards only from its current run

Incident (macbook default board, 2026-11-13): t_01a1d6354e5847dd filed review-nit follow-up
t_01a1d6505a3d1591 (key followup:t_01a1d6354e5847dd:pr2545-nits) in run 42703. A hermes-sync core
update reclaimed that run. The next run (42734) filed the same work again as t_01a1d6546c5bcdd9
(key followup:t_01a1d6354e5847dd:pause-config-bounds). A worker ran on it until a supervisor closed it
as a duplicate. (Card t_01a1d65c3b4d419f.)

Replay of the second create through kanban_overlap._find: the titles share 11 words (ratio 0.61), so
the normal match fires and none of the own_distinct rules separates them. The rule that admitted it is
the idempotency-key family skip `family and card.key_family == family`: both keys are in family
"followup", and the skip runs before the same-creator comparison. The hub caught the same pair only
because replica cards carry no creator.

Fix: that skip no longer covers a card the same creator task made in an EARLIER run
(kanban_overlap_own.earlier_run: card.created_at < the creator's current run start). A keyed batch
filed in one run (rollout:<card>:<leg>, plugin-suites-red:plugins/<name>, review:<card>:<model>:<sha>)
stays exempt, because that run knows every leg. An unknown run start keeps the exemption.

Replay, 14 days, same-creator pairs in one key family with the first card open:
- hub 1721 pairs (71 first-from-earlier-run): before 0 refused, after 1 refused
  (two [selfheal-probe] live checks, a false pair that distinct_from overrides);
- macbook 6 pairs (3 earlier-run): before 0, after 1 (the incident).
Dropping the family skip for every own card instead refused 27 hub pairs, nearly all one-run batch legs.

Tests: the replay fixture gains the incident as a 4th duplicate and 4 same-run keyed fan-outs from the
hub (each first card now carries its real key, so the family skip is exercised); a targeted test shows
the earlier-run pair is refused, while the same pair in one run, or with an unknown run start, is
admitted. RED before the fix: 3 failures (the incident is admitted); GREEN after.
