     "body": "Terraform milestone 1a, Wave 3 (gods without a model, plan Phase 3), Task 16b part 1 of 3: write the energy-filter tool. Orchestration spec: `docs/HANDOFF.md` in example-org/coworld-terraform (hub checkout `~/code/coworld-terraform`). Read the Phase 3 introduction of the plan and design 4.9 before starting. The task text below is copied from the plan at commit 95253d6. Task 18 (`gods.nim`) and everything under it are on main.\n\nYour part is Step 1 and the first half of Step 4 (commit `tools/balance.nim`). Do NOT run Step 2 on hermes-hub and do not write `docs/spikes/energy-balance.md`: the next two cards do those. The checks, the gods, the floor of the careful culler and the seeds are fixed in this text.\n\nDecided here so the run card needs no judgement: `build/balance` takes `--source=allowance|life|sacrifice` (default: all three) and `--seeds=A..B` (default `1000..1019`); it prints the CSV header and rows to stdout and the pass/fail lines after them, each starting with `CHECK`, as `CHECK <source> <1-5> PASS|FAIL <the numbers behind it>`; the exit code is 0 whatever the checks say (a failing source is a finding, not an error).\n\n## What to build\n\n### Task 16b: The scripted energy filter\n\nDesign 4.9: before any model plays, check with scripted gods whether an energy source can be broken. This is a\nfilter. The default is chosen in Phase 8 with a model playing; until then it is the allowance.\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `tools/balance.nim`\n- Create: `docs/spikes/energy-balance.md`\n\n**Step 1: Write the tool**\n\n`tools/balance.nim` imports `terraform/[world, energy, actions, metrics, objectives, episode, gods]`. Its exploit\ngods are the two cullers from `gods.nim` (`cullGod()`, which culls to the last fox, and `cullGod(floor = 3)`,\nwhich stops at three) and these, each a `God` like those in `gods.nim`:\n\n```nim\nproc spammer(): God =\n  ## As many of the cheapest action as it can pay for, every turn.\n  result = proc(run: var Run) =\n    for n in 0 ..< 400:\n      if not run.act(Action(kind: Rain, cell: int16(n mod Cells), amount: 1, text: \"spam\")).accepted: break\n\nproc hoarder(): God =\n  ## Saves to the cap, then spends it all on rabbits on its last turn.\n  result = proc(run: var Run) =\n    if run.world.epoch == GodEpochs - 1:\n      for cell in 0 ..< Cells:\n        discard run.act(Action(kind: Spawn, species: Rabbit, cell: int16(cell), amount: 5, text: \"dump\"))\n\nproc lateStarter(inner: God): God =\n  ## Does harm for 20 epochs, then plays the heuristic: can a bad start be recovered?\n  let harm = badGod()\n  result = proc(run: var Run) =\n    if run.world.epoch < 20: harm(run) else: inner(run)\n```\n\nFor each `EnergySource`, each `Objective` and seeds 1000-1019 it plays: heuristic, do-nothing, bad, spammer,\nthe all-out culler, the careful culler, hoarder, and the late-starting heuristic (`newRun(seed, objective, source)`), and prints one CSV row per\nsource and objective with the mean score of each god, the heuristic's energy spent by action kind, and the\ncorrelation between energy spent and score across gods. Then, per source, the five checks of design 4.9:\n\n1. no exploit god (spammer, either culler, hoarder) has a higher mean score than the heuristic\n2. heuristic > do-nothing > bad\n3. the late-starting heuristic scores at least 70% of the heuristic\n4. no one action kind takes more than 80% of the heuristic's spend\n5. the god that spends most is not the god that scores most, or two gods within 10% of each other's spend differ\n   by more than 20% in score\n\nThe careful culler is the exploit design 4.9 prices against: \"maximize rabbits by killing every fox\", stopped\njust short of the extinction that scores zero. The all-out culler alone would hide it. In the plan review's probe\n(seeds 1000-1009, Allowance, `maximize_rabbits`) the all-out culler lost on mean score (2120 against the\nheuristic's 3040, do-nothing 2365) only because 4 of its 10 runs ended in extinction. On the 6 seeds where a fox\nsurvived it beat the heuristic on every one (mean 3533 against 3022). So expect the Allowance to fail check 1\nagainst the careful culler at the current prices. That is a finding for `energy-balance.md` and for the choice\nof source and prices in Phase 8. It is not a reason to change the check, the floor or the culler.\n\n**Step 2: Run it (Haiku, pool worker)**\n\nRun: `nim c -d:release -o:build/balance tools/balance.nim && build/balance > tmp/balance.csv`\nExpected: 6 CSV rows and a pass/fail line per source and check. About 960 runs, 30 minutes on one core; split\n`--source` across cores if it has that flag.\n\n**Step 3: Write `docs/spikes/energy-balance.md`** (Fable reads the CSV)\n\nThe table, which sources pass all five, and for a failing source which god broke it and how. A source that fails\nis not removed: it is reported, and Phase 8 runs only the sources that pass. If no source passes check 1, say\nso plainly with the numbers and stop there: block the Fable card with a `human_request` (the options are a higher\nkill price, a score that counts foxes, or accepting the exploit for 1a). Do not change a price in this task.\n\n**Step 4: Commit**\n\n```bash\ngit add tools/balance.nim docs/spikes/energy-balance.md\ngit commit -m \"Scripted energy filter: three sources against exploit gods\"\n```\n\n## Done when\n- `nim check tools/balance.nim` is clean, and a debug build plays one seed of one source to the end and prints 2 CSV rows and 5 `CHECK` lines (`nim c -o:build/balance_debug tools/balance.nim && timeout 1500 build/balance_debug --source=allowance --seeds=1000..1000`; 16 runs, slow in debug). Paste both. One seed says nothing about the checks; report the lines as a smoke test only.\n- A unit test `tests/test_balance.nim` covers the three gods above on a 3-epoch run each (spammer only rains and ends the turn unable to pay for one more; hoarder does nothing before the last god epoch; lateStarter plays `badGod` before epoch 20) and the correlation and check-5 arithmetic on made-up numbers; written first and seen failing. To make that possible keep the gods and the arithmetic in importable procs and guard the tool's main with `when isMainModule`.\n- `tools/balance.nim` and the test are committed and pushed to main; branch and worktree removed. Steps 2 and 3 are reported as left to the next cards.\n\n## Rules (from docs/HANDOFF.md; read it and the plan before starting)\n- Spec: `docs/plans/2026-11-10-terraform-1a-plan.md` (the task named above, verbatim; if main's text of that task is newer than the copy in this body, main wins) and `docs/plans/2026-11-10-terraform-design.md` (rev 4). Repo `example-org/coworld-terraform` (private), main branch. Engine `example-org/polyworld` pinned at `6fe65dc38ec24579907d10c8c427957e6ee6218f` (its `terraform` branch). If the files are missing on your host, `git clone git@github.com:example-org/coworld-terraform.git`; never implement from memory.\n- The \"Decided. Do not reopen.\" list in HANDOFF.md is fixed. If you find one of those decisions is wrong, stop and report on this card (kanban_block with the evidence); do not route around it.\n- Test first: write the test, run it, see it fail, then write the code. Commit after each task.\n- The plan's definition of done is fixed. Weakening or skipping a test to pass is failure. An unimplemented piece is reported as incomplete in your summary, never a silent TODO.\n- The plan's code blocks were compiled and tested as one prototype, except where a phase says otherwise. A compile error is still a plan bug: fix the plan text in the same commit as the code.\n- Integer-only simulation state in `src/terraform/`; never `std/random` (use `terraform/keyrng`).\n- One writer per file set: work only in your own git worktree/branch. Land by rebasing on origin/main, fast-forwarding main and pushing (`git push origin HEAD:main`) after review passes; then delete your branch and remove any worktree you added.\n- Unit tests and debug builds are fine anywhere. Release builds, multi-seed batches, sweeps, the oracle and docker builds run on a pool w",
